---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import MiniBoard from '../components/MiniBoard.astro';
import { findPuzzle } from '../games/nonogram/puzzles';
import { cluesFor } from '../games/nonogram/solver';

const lighthouse = findPuzzle('lighthouse')!;
const lc = cluesFor(lighthouse.solution);
---
<Base
  title="Doodle Heroes"
  description="Hand-made logic puzzles you can solve without guessing. Play Island Sketch picture puzzles, learn the techniques from illustrated guides, and try a new daily challenge every day."
  section="home"
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Hand-made logic puzzles</p>
  <h1>Bring the island back, one puzzle at a time.</h1>
  <p class="lede">Doodle Heroes is a small collection of logic puzzles set on a hand-drawn island. Every puzzle has exactly one answer, and every one can be reached by reasoning alone — you never have to guess. Free, no account, and your progress stays in your own browser.</p>

  <section class="card game">
    <div class="game-text">
      <p class="eyebrow">Picture logic</p>
      <h2>Island Sketch</h2>
      <p>Number clues tell you how many squares to fill in each row and column. Work them out and a sketch of the island appears: the lighthouse, the harbour, the old volcano. Twelve hand-drawn puzzles in three chapters, plus a fresh puzzle every day.</p>
      <p class="actions">
        <a class="btn" href="/island-sketch/play?p=sun">Play the first sketch ▶</a>
        <a class="btn secondary" href="/island-sketch">Rules &amp; all puzzles</a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="game-art">
      <MiniBoard rows={lc.rows} cols={lc.cols} state={lighthouse.solution.map((r) => r.replaceAll('.', '?'))} label="A solved Island Sketch puzzle showing a lighthouse" cell={20} />
      <figcaption>“The Lighthouse”, solved.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <h2>Today’s challenge</h2>
  <p>Each day brings a new 10 × 10 <a href="/island-sketch/play?p=daily">daily sketch</a>, the same for everyone. It’s generated from the date, then checked by a solver to make sure it can be finished without guessing.</p>

  <h2>Learn to solve, not just to play</h2>
  <p>Logic puzzles are more fun once you can see the reasoning. Our guides teach the techniques with real board positions, and the walkthroughs solve a full puzzle step by step.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/guides/nonogram-basics">Picture logic for beginners</a> — the overlap rule, full lines and edges.</li>
    <li><a href="/guides/nonogram-advanced">Advanced picture logic</a> — reach, gaps, and matching squares to blocks.</li>
    <li><a href="/walkthroughs/the-lighthouse">Walkthrough: The Lighthouse</a> — a complete 10 × 10 solve.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The in-game Hint button uses the same reasoning: rather than revealing a random square, it shows you the row or column where the next deduction is, so you can work it out yourself.</p>

  <h2>How we make the puzzles</h2>
  <p>Every picture is drawn by hand on a grid, then run through a solver that only uses the kind of one-line reasoning a person can do. If the solver can’t finish a puzzle without guessing, the picture is redrawn until it can. You can read more <a href="/about">about the project</a>.</p>
</Base>

<style>
  .game{ display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:18px 26px; align-items:center; }
  .game-text{ flex:1 1 300px; }
  .game-text h2{ margin-top:0; }
  .game-art{ flex:0 1 260px; margin:0 auto; }
  .actions{ display:flex; gap:10px; flex-wrap:wrap; margin-bottom:0; }
</style>
